  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes different kinds of chemical bonding and interactions. The top section presents the hard–soft acid–base idea: small, high charge-density species are “hard” acids and bases, while larger, more polarizable species are “soft.” It shows that hard acids preferentially bind hard bases, and soft acids bind soft bases. A few familiar ions and molecules are given as examples, such as H+, Na+, F−, OH−, Cu+, Au+, iodide, and sulfur-containing ligands.\n\nThe lower part contrasts bonding patterns in three types of substances. One example is sodium chloride, drawn as an extended lattice in which each atom is surrounded by six oppositely charged neighbors, emphasizing ionic crystal packing. Another is argon, shown as closely packed spheres with about twelve nearest neighbors per atom, illustrating a structure held together by weak, non-directional interactions rather than true chemical bonds. The third is dichloromethane, depicted as a discrete molecular species in which carbon forms four directional covalent bonds to two chlorines and two hydrogens.\n\nTaken together, the figure compares how ionic, van der Waals, and covalent interactions differ in selectivity, coordination, and structural organization.",
